Ran the pass against §10 and the proposed §18 with the three constraints applied. Seven findings, no feature proposals. Two of them are defects in my own S-Tier drafts, which is the outcome I wanted from doing this before the cut rather than after.

F1 — Class A has no gate representation. Hard defect, and §17.1 instantiates it. §4 emits gates as XML literals into a Workflow/Gates section. §8.2 Class A forbids XML literals and prose outright. §16.1 ships gate semantics to every class. So a Class A artifact with an unresolved Tier-1 parameter must either carry an XML literal (violates §8.2) or carry no gate (violates GATE COMPLETENESS). §17.1 — the calibration example for the most common machine-readable invocation — says "emitted as blocking gate G1" and is therefore uncompilable. §6.4 and §4's coverage-gap clause both solved this for their own payloads and neither generalized. Observable: a Class A artifact containing an XML literal, or a Tier-1 gap with no gate object. No existing check detects it; SCHEMA CONFORMANCE reads the user's schema, not the envelope. Needs a "gates": [...] routing clause in §4 plus a new check.

F2 — QUALITY-LAYER TRIGGER false-positives on a legal configuration. §6.3 PASS SELECTION permits an empty {B,C,D} where Pass B is not mandatory, reducing to draft-only, and the Compile Header has a draft-only value for exactly this. §10 fails if the protocol is "absent with one in Class B." Ratified rubric + Class B + draft-only is legal and halts on VERIFICATION_FAILED. Existing defect, not introduced by v1.3.

F3 — PILLAR STATUS is unenforceable as I drafted it. It orders five statuses, but §3 defines only three. MISSING and CONFLICTED have no definitions anywhere in v1.2, so "the lowest status among that pillar's own items" is not computable and two operators tier the same pillar differently. My own check fails the observable test I wrote the registry to apply. §3 must define all five before this ships.

F4 — SCOPE CONFORMANCE is narrower than its invariant. I scoped clause (b) to §7 APPLICABILITY. The same defect exists elsewhere: §4's Class A coverage_gaps routing governs no reachable case, because the sole non-blocking gate source is §6.1 rule 3, which is Class B only, and Pass C is Class B only. Compile-time-only error codes shipping under §11 are a third instance. The invariant is the §16.1 shipping rule generally; the check as drafted detects one instance of it.

F5 — Tier-3 liveness deadlock. SUFFICIENT admits a pillar whose only open items are Tier-3. §3.3 forbids asking Tier-3 questions. §3 requires 1–3 questions per turn, §3.3 requires strict reduction, §3.1 permits compile only on all-ESTABLISHED or a user election. In that state there is no legal turn and no legal compile, and the compiler may not suggest an exit. Cleanest repair is to stop treating Tier-3 as open — it is resolved at classification.

F6 — I9 governs no reachable case in v1.3. §3.3 requires candidates be "drawn from the user's own supplied material... never invented as business fact." Compiler-originated candidate lists are currently prohibited, so a check policing their menu structure has nothing to fire on until the A-Tier candidate-origin framework lands. Either amend §3.3 in v1.3 or demote I9 to A-Tier. Shipping it now reproduces F4's defect class in a brand-new entry.

F7 — three consolidations into existing checks. "ratified" as a v1.3 provenance value collides with §15.2's closed three-label vocabulary, where it must route as unprovenanced — widen PROVENANCE MAPPING rather than add a check. §17.4 asserts a §10 failure that no §10 check names — widen CROSS-REFERENCE to cover assertions about a target's contents, not just its existence. §16.1 machinery containment and §16.4's mandatory prohibition are wholly unenforced; that one does need a check, since leaked machinery is defective precisely because it is consumable, which SCOPE CONFORMANCE cannot see.

text
  PILLAR STATUS         — blocked pending §3 definitions of MISSING and CONFLICTED
  QUALITY-LAYER TRIGGER   ...absent with one in Class B, except where §6.3 reduces to
                        draft-only and the Compile Header records quality passes =
                        draft-only; or present in Class A or C; or...
  SCOPE CONFORMANCE     fails if a gate's ON_MISSING names an operation the output class
                        cannot partially withhold (§8.2); or if any rule, routing branch,
                        or error code ships that no path reachable in the compiled
                        artifact can consume (§16.1 shipping rule).
  REPRESENTATION        (Class A only) fails if any XML literal appears in the artifact,
                        or if a gate, coverage gap, proposal, or compile header appears
                        outside its metadata object.
  LIVENESS              (slow only) fails if any reachable session state admits no
                        permissible question, no decomposition, and no compile condition.
  MACHINERY CONTAINMENT fails if the artifact contains content from a section §16.1
                        enumerates as pure compile-time; or if Output Rules omits the
                        §16.4 non-re-entrancy prohibition.
  REFERENCE INTEGRITY   (was CROSS-REFERENCE) fails if any §n reference resolves to a
                        wrong or absent section, or asserts a check, error code, or rule
                        not present at the target.
  PROVENANCE MAPPING      ...or if a field carrying a provenance label outside §15.2's
                        three-label set was adopted rather than routed as unprovenanced.

v1.3 is blocked on three text fixes, not on any open decision: §4 needs Class A gate routing, §3 needs the two missing status definitions, and §3.3 needs either amendment or an I9 demotion. All three are repairs to defects, not new capability.

Registry goes 9 → 12; §10 goes to six compile-time checks plus one revision-scoped. That is more growth than I'd like, and each new entry states why no existing check can carry it — F7's first two deliberately don't add checks.

One meta-observation, since it bears on how much more of this to do: a single pass produced three new invariants and two defects in checks drafted last round. The registry is under-populated by more than I estimated, and the highest-yield finder so far is not invariant hunting but auditing whether a named check detects its stated observable — F3 and F4 both came from that, and both were mine. I'd cut v1.3 with the repairs above rather than delay for another pass, then run that audit as a standing step on every registry entry.
